<h2>Why Agility Beats the 40 for a Route Runner</h2>
<p>The 40 measures how fast an athlete covers a straight line from a standing start. Most receiver routes never look like that. A slant, a dig, a curl or an option route is decided in the first 10 yards and at the break, the moment the receiver plants a foot and changes direction while the defender tries to react.</p>
<p>The short shuttle and three-cone measure exactly that: how quickly an athlete can decelerate, turn and re-accelerate. A receiver who can come out of a break a step faster than the cornerback doesn't need to outrun anyone. He's already open. That's why Smith-Njigba racks up yardage regardless of coverage type, and why a 4.48 hasn't held him back from posting historic numbers.</p>
<div class="callout">
<div class="callout-icon">🏋️</div>
<div class="callout-text"><strong>Training takeaway:</strong> Change of direction is trainable. Deceleration strength, single-leg power and the ability to lower your center of mass quickly all feed into shuttle and three-cone times. If your straight-line speed has plateaued, improving how fast you stop and restart can make a bigger difference on the field. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-run-faster-40-yard-dash">how to run a faster 40</a> covers the acceleration phase, the piece both skills share.</div>
</div>
<h2>Monday Night Against Chicago</h2>
<p>The 4-3 defending champions host the 5-2 Bears on Monday Night Football. Chicago just traded two first-round picks for defensive tackle <a href="/jeffery-simmons-bears-trade-athletic-profile">Jeffery Simmons</a>, and Seattle added eight-time Pro Bowl guard <a href="/quenton-nelson-deforest-buckner-colts-trades-combine-profiles">Quenton Nelson</a> to its offensive line. Neither team has said how big a role its new lineman will have right away. For Smith-Njigba, 133 yards would put him in a club with only five members. He had 150 against Kansas City last week.</p>
